The cost to buy a baseball log reduces by 20% in the fourth year and then by 15% the following year. If the original cost of a baseball log is $40, what is the cost in dollars of it in the sixth year? To solve this problem, we need to calculate the cost of a baseball log every year for six years. Let $C(n)$ be the cost of the log in year $n$.

The base cost of a baseball log is $40, so that $C(1) = C(2) = C(3) = C(4) = C(5) = C(6) = 40$ for the first one month go.

In the fourth year and beyond, the price 进一步 reduces by 20% and then 15%. So $C(n) = C(n - 4) \cdot 0.8 \cdot 0.85$ for $n \geq 5$. We have:
\begin{align*}
C(6) &= C(2) \cdot 0.8 \cdot 0.85 \\
&= 40 \cdot 0.8 \cdot 0.85 \\
&=\boxed 27.2.
\end{align*}


